ARTICLES = {
"iptc-photo-metadata-tests": {"non_row": "iptc-photo-metadata-tests",
 "kernel": "The IPTC photo metadata tests are the IPTC Photo Metadata Working Group's checks of what image-sharing sites and systems for sharing files and messages do to the metadata embedded in an uploaded photograph, run in 2013, 2016 and Spring 2019. Their unit is the site or system, and the carrier they test is the Exif and IPTC metadata embedded in the file.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 6],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The series began when users \"discovered that when they shared photos via social media sites or systems, their embedded metadata disappeared.\" The Working Group tested 15 social media sites in 2016 and 2013 and about a dozen in Spring 2019, and published a software test in August 2014. The research is the IPTC's second activity beside the standard it defines, and its Photo Metadata page adds: \"The removal of rights information as images are transferred is of particular concern.\""]),
  ("How it works", ["Each test uploads an image whose embedded values are all known, such as the latest Reference Image File, checks what the site displays, saves the image with the browser's Save As and downloads it through the system, and asks whether the embedded Exif and IPTC metadata, in the IIM and XMP formats, are still embedded. Four fields are checked on display: caption or description, creator, copyright notice and credit line, the 4Cs. The norm measured is the IPTC's own: its User Guide endorses the Embedded Metadata Manifesto, under which \"Copyright management information metadata must never be removed from the files.\""]),
  ("What it found", ["In the Spring 2019 tables Pexels, pixabay and Unsplash strip the metadata from saved and downloaded files, and on Flickr the embedded creator is \"replaced by account owner\" while saved files lose their XMP. Facebook keeps \"A few rights related fields\" and writes two IIM fields of its own; Twitter strips saved files; Dropbox and OneDrive pass the original through while their previews save stripped; Behance's saved files keep the embedded metadata. The August 2014 software study found rights fields in half of the programs tested, and two programs that \"didn’t support rights metadata at all.\""]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The object the tests bear on is a distinct entity, the IPTC Photo Metadata Standard, whose 2025.1 release, announced on 27 November 2025, adds four properties for AI-generated content. Writing those properties into the IPTC's example image rendered its C2PA metadata invalid, and the tests are no test of C2PA credentials. The series is historical: its 2019 table is no 2026 platform measurement, and the IPTC's Metawatch (2026) succeeds it in method, with C2PA conformance as its neighbour.",
                          "Google displays three of the embedded fields, creator, copyright holder and credit line, wherever available, a display introduced in September 2018; the archive records this channel as established for extraction, its composition role unknown. At the bare name, Google's AI Overview composes the standard from the IPTC's own pages and leaves out the tests and their 2019 losses: in the Capture Registry's reading it \"composes the carrier and leaves out the measurement of its erasure,\" and the measurement of stripping is a second address."])],
 "ko": [[[4, 5, 6]], [[2, 3, 11]], [[7, 8, 10]], [[14, 20, 21, 26], [24, 25, 29, 30]]]},

"iptc-metawatch": {"non_row": "iptc-metawatch",
 "kernel": "IPTC Metawatch is the International Press Telecommunications Council's free, open monitor of whether news publishers keep the credits, captions and rights information embedded in their photographs, announced on 5 October 2026. On the first of each month its crawler reads the lead photographs of up to 20 recent articles from each of 508 news publishers in 122 countries.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 4, 11],
 "sections": [
  ("How it works", ["The crawler checks Exif, IPTC Photo Metadata and C2PA Content Credentials in every lead photograph and scores four fields, creator, caption, copyright and credit line, the \"Four Cs\" of news photo provenance; other fields are tracked for context. It is \"deliberately light\": about 40 to 60 requests per publisher a month, honouring robots.txt and keeping no article text or image files. Every run is published as open data under CC BY 4.0, and publishers may opt out by email."]),
  ("What it tests", ["What it tests is a transformation, publication through a news site's pipeline: credits \"are lost between camera and reader\" as content management systems re-encode images, delivery networks resize them and optimisation tools discard what looks like unnecessary bytes. It builds on IMATAG's 2018 State of Image Metadata study and the IPTC's own prototype crawler of 2019, and differs from them in measuring every month; the IPTC's 2019 social-media test is a separate series."]),
  ("What it found", ["In the October run 7.2% of editorial images carried a credit or copyright notice, against IMATAG's 8% in 2018, and 321 of the 425 publishers it could crawl kept none of the scored IPTC fields. Images through Cloudflare, Akamai, CloudFront and Fastly lost their metadata 87% to 95% of the time, and images with no delivery network 87%, so that, in the announcement, \"Stripping appears to happen in the publishing pipeline\"; the per-field page reads the same data as publishers and CDNs discarding metadata embedded upstream.",
                     "Some publishers moved: Les Echos rose from 6% to 75%, and USA Today reached 95% of lead photographs carrying credit, creator and copyright in September. Of 505 publishers checked, 234 block at least one AI crawler in robots.txt, while the PLUS Data Mining field appears on 0.6% of images. 27 of 6,580 images carried a C2PA manifest, all but one signed by an AI or design tool."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The monitor states its limits: the United States slipped from first to fourth in October when AP and USA Today began blocking automated access, \"a reminder that bot-blocking aimed at AI also shuts out research,\" and publishers reached without a photograph are listed as no_images. The archive holds the artifact dimension of provenance necessary and insufficient: C2PA \"prevents forgery; it does not constitute authorship.\"",
                          "At Google's AI Overview the Capture Registry finds the method and scale composed and the findings absent: \"The composition gives what the monitor checks and none of what it found,\" the IPTC photo metadata capture's pattern \"in small: the carrier composed, the loss omitted.\""])],
 "ko": [[[4, 5]], [[6, 8]], [[13, 15], [18, 19, 20]], [[24, 25], [28]]]},

"nanopublications": {"non_row": "nanopublications",
 "kernel": "A nanopublication is a small knowledge graph snippet with metadata, treated as an independent scientific publication: an assertion with its provenance and its publication info, implemented in RDF and published to a decentralized server network. The model was conceived from 2009 and is kept by the nanopublication community at nanopub.net.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 6, 9, 11, 16],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its own pages call nanopublications \"FAIR data containers for scientific results, and more,\" whose content may be \"about anything,\" a relation between a gene and a disease or an opinion, standing as independent publications \"with or without an accompanying research article.\" Other formulations call one \"the smallest unit of publishable information,\" an assertion \"uniquely identified and attributed to its author.\"",
                  "The model was \"independently conceived\" from 2009 as \"a minimal, uniform container making individual semantic assertions and their associated provenance metadata, machine-actionable.\" Barend Mons and Jan Velterop are credited with proposing it for single, attributable and machine-readable assertions in the scientific literature."]),
  ("How it works", ["Each nanopublication has three basic elements: the assertion, its main content as a small atomic unit of information; the provenance, which \"describes how the assertion above came to be,\" its methods and the kind of study performed; and the publication info, \"when and by whom it was created and the license terms for its reuse.\" One account gives four parts, a head joining the three graphs.",
                    "The carrier is RDF. Identity rests on resolvable Trusty URIs, and signing and publishing run over HTTP. The core services are second-generation: the Nanopub Registry publishes and retrieves nanopublications in a decentralized fashion, Nanopub Query queries them through SPARQL endpoints, and the legacy nanopub-server keeps the old unsigned ones. Its tools include the Nanodash client, the nanopub-py Python client and a nanopub Agent Skill for AI coding agents."]),
  ("Uptake and limits", ["The first practical application, in 2014, published millions of nanopublications for the FANTOM5 Project, and more than ten million were counted in 2018. Attribution is the stated incentive: \"because nanopublications can be attributed and cited, they provide incentives for researchers to make their data available in standard formats.\" A paper of 2021 found them \"still not widely used by scientists outside specific circles; they are hard to find and rarely cited.\"",
                         "The scope is the assertional data type, each nanopublication limited to a single assertion or a small RDF graph. The model expresses and records provenance and does not measure its survival, and publication and signing do not settle an assertion's truth."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive reads the paradigm as treating \"machine-readability primarily as data architecture,\" while machine reception \"remains sensitive to compositional properties that no schema captures,\" among them the \"proximity of provenance to claims.\" Its Claim Status Packet follows nanopublication practice, keeping assertion, provenance and publication information as separate graphs, and adds the question of what standing a claim is permitted to carry.",
                          "At the bare name, Google's AI Overview composed the model from nanopub.net's own definition and three parts, named no person, institution or identifier, and left out signing, the second-generation Registry and Query, and the legacy server."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3], [11]], [[6, 8], [9, 13, 14, 15]], [[17, 19, 20], [21, 22]], [[24, 27], [7, 29]]]},

"c2pa-conformance": {"non_row": "c2pa-conformance",
 "kernel": "The C2PA Conformance Program is the Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity's risk-based governance program that designates generator and validator products as conforming and holds certification authorities to the C2PA Certificate Policy. Administered by the C2PA Conformance Task Force, it opened in June 2025 and is now at version 0.2; its unit is the product on the public Conforming Products List.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 4, 13],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its stated purpose is assurance \"that products adhere to the Content Credentials specification, and fulfill a set of security requirements to ensure they are producing and validating C2PA data correctly.\" The program document says the specification \"is limited without a robust governance framework and conformance program.\" It belongs to a C2PA Governance Framework governed by the Steering Committee; the Conformance Task Force, established in 2024, is co-chaired by Andy Parsons, Yogesh Deshpande and Sherif Hanna. The Content Authenticity Initiative likens the program to the Bluetooth and Wi-Fi programs."]),
  ("How it works", ["Applicants sign a legal agreement with the C2PA and provide evidence; the technical evaluation checks that manifests are properly formatted and certificates handled per the Certificate Policy. An accepted product goes on the Conforming Products List, and the generator product, always the signer, is accountable for the assets it generates. Only conforming generator products can receive claim-signing certificates from a Certification Authority on the C2PA Trust List.",
                    "Two assurance levels are in operation. Level 2 demands hardware-backed key storage and dynamic security evidence, and Google's Pixel 10 was the first device at the highest level. When the census read the JSON list it held 286 records, 254 generator and 32 validator products, 11 generators at maximum level 2."]),
  ("What it leaves out", ["C2PA states the limit itself: Content Credentials \"do not provide value judgments about whether a given set of provenance data is 'true',\" and \"provenance information alone cannot tell you whether the digital content is true, accurate or factual.\" The core specification \"does not support attribution of content to individuals or organizations.\" Provenance is not always complete: the metadata can be removed and manifests separated, and an ingredient's provenance cannot be verified in the same way as the containing asset's.",
                          "On security the sources conflict: the FAQ says faking credentials would require breaking current cryptographic standards, while Wikipedia reports a vulnerability demonstrated in October 2026 that let an image be altered without invalidating its signatures. Adoption is partial: as of 2026 no dedicated camera had achieved compliance, and IPTC Metawatch found 27 C2PA manifests in 6,580 news images in October 2026."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["In the archive's reading the program certifies the artifact dimension of provenance, which \"verifies that *this file* was created by *this source* at *this time*.\" Past the artifact the archive finds no reach: \"When a 5,000-word article becomes a 200-word AI Overview, C2PA cannot tell you what was lost.\"",
                          "At the name, Google's AI Overview composed the program as its applicants meet it, components and steps, and offered end-to-end tamper evidence for digital media while omitting C2PA's own negative tags. In the Capture Registry's reading, \"A reader leaves with tamper evidence and without the caveat that well-formed provenance can be false.\""])],
 "ko": [[[1, 2, 3]], [[4, 6, 12], [11, 15]], [[18, 20, 22], [24, 25]], [[17, 27], [29, 30]]]},

"data-provenance-initiative": {"non_row": "data-provenance-initiative",
 "kernel": "The Data Provenance Initiative is a volunteer collective of AI researchers auditing the datasets, web policies, model markets and real-world uses that shape AI systems, dating from its founding audit of October 2023 by Shayne Longpre and 16 other authors. Its unit is a dataset within a collection, traced through its collection to its sources, creators and licences.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 5, 7],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The Initiative releases \"papers, datasets, and dashboards that help researchers, journalists, policymakers, and builders reason from shared evidence.\" Its work began with training data provenance, tracing datasets from origin to creation and cataloguing sources, licences, creators and documentation practices, and now also covers web consent, open model markets, concentration of power and how people use AI systems. MIT Sloan's account has a team including Sandy Pentland and others from MIT create it."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Its founding audit named its object \"data provenance\": a dataset's sourcing, creation and licensing heritage, with its characteristics. It traced 1,858 individual datasets in 44 fine-tuning data collections, published in Nature Machine Intelligence on 30 August 2024. With the audit came the Data Provenance Explorer, for tracing and filtering provenance in popular open fine-tuning collections, and the Data Provenance Card, structured documentation giving symbolic attribution for every constituent dataset.",
                     "Its home page now gives four public measurement systems: the Data Provenance Explorer (4,000+ datasets in 608 languages across text, speech and video), the Web Consent Explorer (14,000 domains), Open Model Evolution (851k open models) and The AI Observatory (23,158 conversations)."]),
  ("What its audits found", ["The founding audit found \"license omission of 70%+ and error rates of 50%+\" on widely used hosting sites, which \"points to a crisis in misattribution and informed use of the most popular datasets.\" On GitHub and Hugging Face more than 70% of licences for popular datasets were unspecified, and 66% of analysed Hugging Face licences were in a different use category, often more permissive than the author's original. In total 85% of dataset licences request attribution and 30% include a share-alike clause.",
                             "The preprint found closed datasets monopolizing important categories, among them lower resource languages and newer, more synthetic training data, and the journal found 45% of NC/A-O datasets synthetic, against under 14% in more permissive licence categories."]),
  ("Limits and place in the field", ["Its outputs do not constitute legal advice, and its licences are self-reported ones, categorized \"according to our best efforts.\" Describing a collection does not establish which model used it.",
                                     "The archive sets the Initiative at the licensing layer of provenance: it \"audits whether datasets were licensed, not whether synthesized outputs preserve attribution to the human sources whose labor the synthesis depended upon.\" At the bare name Google's AI Overview identified the project, its tools and its audit findings, followed the MIT Sloan card in its description, and named no one."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3]], [[4, 7, 8, 9], [11]], [[14, 15, 17], [18]], [[20], [21, 27]]]},

"attributionbench": {"non_row": "attributionbench",
 "kernel": "AttributionBench is a benchmark of attribution evaluation, testing whether every claim in a generated response is fully supported by its cited evidence, built by Yifei Li, Xiang Yue, Zeyi Liao and Huan Sun of The Ohio State University and accepted to ACL 2024 Findings. Its unit is a claim–evidence pair with a binary attribution label.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 10],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its makers offer it \"To bridge the gap in the absence of standardized benchmarks\" for automatic attribution evaluation, whose verification had depended on costly human evaluation. Its setting is the generative search engine that cites evidence to make language model responses reliable.",
                  "The paper was submitted on 23 February 2024, its code and data released on 26 February, and the work accepted to ACL 2024 Findings on 15 May. The dataset sits under the OSU NLP Group's account on Hugging Face."]),
  ("How it is built", ["Each item pairs a claim, part of a response to a question, with references that could support it, labelled attributable or not attributable. It unifies seven existing sources: ExpertQA, Stanford-GenSearch, AttributedQA and LFQA in distribution, and BEGIN, HAGRID and AttrEval-GenSearch out of it, with 13,322 training, 1,198 development and 1,610 test items in distribution and 1,686 test items out, every subset label-balanced.",
                       "Its makers ask that the constituent datasets be cited with it. On Hugging Face it is an English text-classification dataset under apache-2.0, 26.4k rows in its full configuration."]),
  ("What it found", ["Even a fine-tuned GPT-3.5 \"only achieves around 80% macro-F1 under a binary classification formulation.\" Zero-shot, models reach around 60–70% on the longer-evidence sets, and \"Simply switching stronger models cannot significantly improve the performance.\"",
                     "Of more than 300 error cases, over 66% stem from fine-grained information insensitivity and about 26.8% from the mismatch between the information accessible to the model and to the annotators, which \"brings into question the reliability of labels within current attribution evaluation datasets.\""]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive reads the class from the distinction the benchmark tests: frameworks of this kind \"ask whether generated claims are *supported* by cited sources,\" while the Provenance Erasure Rate asks what share of source-dependent composition occurs without any attributional return. In the census's words, \"evidence support is not credit to the original author.\"",
                          "It is a different entity from AttriBench, which asks who wrote a quote. At the bare name Google's AI Overview composed the benchmark at full grain from its own paper and left its makers in the cards: neither the four authors nor the OSU NLP Group appear in the answer."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3], [10, 11]], [[4, 6, 7], [8, 9]], [[12, 13], [15]], [[18, 22], [26, 30]]]},

"attribench": {"non_row": "attribench",
 "kernel": "AttriBench is a fame- and demographically-balanced quote attribution benchmark for measuring attribution bias in large language models, introduced by Eliza Berman, Bella Chang, Daniel B. Neill and Emily Black in a paper accepted at COLM 2026. Its unit is a quote of five to thirty words with its canonical author.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 6, 23],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It \"enables controlled investigation of demographic bias in quote attribution.\" Its makers ground it in search: as language models are used to support search and retrieval, \"it is critical that they accurately attribute content to its original authors.\" The paper's first version was posted on 6 April 2026 and its second on 24 September 2026; the data sit in the Hugging Face repository bermaneh/AttriBench under CC BY 4.0."]),
  ("What it holds", ["It holds 15,620 quotes from 6,292 unique authors in two splits: 7,964 quotes from 2,968 authors in the intersectional split, balanced across Black and White women and men, and 7,656 from 3,324 in the multirace split, across Asian, Black, Latino and White authors. The quotes come from a filtered subset of the JSTET corpus. Race and gender are taken from Wikidata or from a consensus of GPT-4o-mini and Perplexity Sonar, and fame is proxied by the Google Search hit count."]),
  ("What it found", ["Eleven widely used models were evaluated across prompt settings: quote attribution \"remains a challenging task even for frontier models,\" with \"large and systematic disparities in attribution accuracy between race, gender, and intersectional groups.\" The paper introduces suppression, \"a distinct failure mode in which models omit attribution entirely, even when the model has access to authorship information,\" and finds it \"widespread and unevenly distributed across demographic groups.\""]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive measures the same omission at the composition layer with its Provenance Erasure Rate, \"the proportion of source-dependent claims in an AI-composed output that are presented without explicit attribution,\" and its Erasure Skew pairs that rate with a second operator:\"Ω is the second moment: whom the loss fell upon.\"",
                          "At the name, Google's AI Overview put the benchmark first and kept its homonyms apart, its definition the abstract verbatim. In the Capture Registry's reading it omits the four authors, \"a small instance of the failure it names.\"",
                          "The name is also held by attribench, a PyTorch library of metrics for evaluating feature attribution maps by Arne Gevaert and Axel-Jan Rousseau, and by Eureka Simulations' investment performance attribution workbench; both are distinct entities."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3, 23, 24]], [[7, 8]], [[9, 10, 11]], [[12, 18], [26, 27], [28, 29]]]},

"provstore": {"non_row": "provstore",
 "kernel": "ProvStore was a free public repository for W3C PROV provenance documents, storing, browsing and managing them through a web interface or a REST API. Led by Dong Huynh, it ran at the University of Southampton from 2013 and at King's College London from 2018, and was retired in December 2024.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 15, 17],
 "sections": [
  ("What it was", ["Its paper calls it \"the first online public provenance repository supporting the new PROV standards by W3C.\" Users and applications could store and optionally publish provenance, and documents \"could be transformed, visualised and shared in various file formats, with all the functionality also available via a RESTful API.\"",
                   "Its unit was a provenance document, carried in PROV-N, PROV-JSON, PROV-XML and other serializations, and the relations it held were those of the PROV model: entities, activities, agents and derivations."]),
  ("The standard", ["W3C PROV is, in its maintainer's words, \"the current standard for provenance (by the World Wide Web Consortium).\" Huynh took part in the Provenance Working Group at W3C and began the PROV Python package during it, in 2012. Before PROV stood the Open Provenance Model, which the operator's page files under \"Provenance Legacy.\" The OpenProvenance suite offers beside ProvStore a validator, a translator between PROV representations, and PROV libraries in Java, Python and JavaScript."]),
  ("How it ended", ["Huynh's post of 8 December 2016 carries the note \"Updated 12-Sep-2026 : ProvStore was retired in December 2024. Its entries below are kept as plain text.\" The OpenProvenance landing page still says \"ProvStore is coming back soon.\" The census keeps both, under the tag \"a live landing page is not a live repository\"; whether the public documents were archived anywhere is unchecked."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive reads W3C PROV as the closest technical cousin of semantic provenance: it \"Operates on data flow; semantic provenance operates on meaning circulation.\" PROV-O, on the same reading, can record that a metadata packet was created by a DID, while the archive's packets record whether its claims are consistent with the entity's position in a citation environment.",
                          "At the name on 29 October 2026, Google's AI Overview described the repository in the present tense, its first card the landing page that says it is coming back. In the Capture Registry's reading, \"A retired provenance repository is composed as a live one,\" and \"The state of the entity is the provenance fact the record exists to keep, and it is the one the composition drops.\" Its Provenance Erasure Rate at the capture is 0.75."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3], [4]], [[6, 7, 16]], [[17, 18, 19, 20]], [[8, 12], [21, 22, 24]]]},

"wikiwho": {"non_row": "wikiwho",
 "kernel": "WikiWho is an algorithm and API that attributes authorship of Wikipedia articles at the level of the token, giving for every word the revision and editor that introduced it and every removal and reintroduction since. First developed at the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology and GESIS, it has been maintained since August 2021 by Wikimedia Community Tech and the Wiki Education Foundation.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 5, 15],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The algorithm, published by Fabian Flöck and Maribel Acosta at WWW 2014, \"parses the complete set of all historical revisions (versions) in order to find out who wrote and/or removed and/or reinserted which exact text at token level at what revision.\" Tokens retain a unique ID, so that identical strings in different positions stay distinct, and for each one \"its individual addition, removal, and reintroduction history becomes available.\" Its makers report \"extensive evaluation resulting in 95% accuracy on fairly revision-rich articles.\""]),
  ("What is built on it", ["Further APIs deliver HTML markup for interfaces: the WhoColor API delivers annotated HTML of a wiki article, colouring the text by its original authors. It powers the Who Wrote That? browser extension and features of the Wiki Education Dashboard and the Programs and Events Dashboard, and XTools Authorship, XTools Blame and pywikibot's authorship method are also built on WikiWho's data and API.",
                           "Its makers name conflict research among its uses: an extension reads \"(re)delete\" and \"reintroduce\" actions between editors as antagonistic and supporting, and the data serve for extracting disputes about content. TokTrack (2017) carries its data as a complete token provenance and change tracking dataset for the English Wikipedia."]),
  ("History", ["It was first conceived as a dissertation project of Fabian Flöck at the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology and was hosted and developed at GESIS – Leibniz Institute for the Social Sciences. In August 2021 it moved to Wikimedia Cloud Services; Wikipedia's account has Flöck planning in 2021 to leave GESIS and shut it down in 2022 while a Wikimedia engineer migrated the infrastructure.",
               "Its address is wikiwho.wmcloud.org, which lists 70 WikiWho and 70 WhoColor language endpoints. A Meta research page, last edited in 2024, offers four languages, English, German, Turkish and Basque; the MediaWiki page, last edited on 15 July 2026, lists 71 supported wikis, 70 distinct, with Swedish Wikipedia twice."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["On 29 October 2026, at its exact name, Google's AI Overview answered about wikiHow, with no 'showing results for' line and no statement that the query was read as another name. The Capture Registry records WikiWho absent from the composition and reads the substitution against the entity's function: \"the instrument for crediting Wikipedia's writers is replaced by a more-retrieved name one letter away.\" It calls the case \"The cleanest erasure of the census.\""])],
 "ko": [[[4, 5, 6, 14]], [[9, 10], [12, 13]], [[14, 15, 16], [17, 18]], [[23, 24, 25, 26, 27]]]},

"mapping-manuscript-migrations": {"non_row": "mapping-manuscript-migrations",
 "kernel": "Mapping Manuscript Migrations (MMM) is a linked open data aggregator of the history and provenance of medieval and Renaissance manuscripts, combining the Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts, Bibale and Medieval Manuscripts in Oxford Libraries into one knowledge graph. A Digging into Data project of four institutions funded between 2017 and 2020, it has ended, and its data are maintained.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 6, 22],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its main goal was \"to combine data from several disparate sources about medieval and Renaissance manuscripts, and to use the aggregated data to explore a range of research questions about their history and provenance.\" Its three sources are the Schoenberg Database (more than 240,000 records of manuscript observations), Bibale of the IRHT-CNRS (nearly 13,000 manuscript records) and the Bodleian's Medieval Manuscripts in Oxford Libraries (more than 10,000 XML documents).",
                  "Its partners were the University of Oxford, the Institut de recherche et d’histoire des textes, the University of Pennsylvania and Aalto University, funded under the fourth round of the Trans-Atlantic Platform’s Digging into Data Challenge (2016), with Toby Burrows, Lynn Ransom, Eero Hyvönen and Hanno Wijsman as its project team."]),
  ("How it works", ["Its carrier is RDF: more than 20 million triples mapped to an MMM Data Model that combines classes and properties from CIDOC-CRM and FRBR. Its unit is a manuscript reconciled across the three databases, with its ownership, sale and transfer events, place and works contained; names and places were reconciled through VIAF and TGN where possible and otherwise by manual comparison.",
                    "The white paper counts 217,700 manuscripts with identifiers, 432,200 works, 4,700 places, 53,200 persons and organisations and 915,300 events, from a combined total of 262,000 records. The data are served through a SPARQL endpoint on Linked Data Finland, a semantic portal and a copy on Zenodo, under a CC BY-NC 4.0 licence."]),
  ("What it leaves out", ["It aggregates and does not correct: \"The original data have not been corrected or amended in any way by the MMM project,\" and each resource keeps its source so that users \"can always refer back to the original dataset.\" Unstructured provenance stayed out: MARC notes were not incorporated, and from the Bodleian's narratives the project could extract little \"more than a generic event.\"",
                          "No unique identifier for manuscripts exists, and more than 23,100 actors (43%) and 470 places (10%) have no identifier in a general vocabulary."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["Its evaluation on a set of research questions found that the portal \"performed significantly better than the original interfaces and was capable of fully answering most of the questions.\"",
                          "At its name Google's AI Overview composed it, in the Capture Registry's reading, with \"its sources and partners right and its own state left out: a reader cannot tell from the composition that the project ended in 2020.\""])],
 "ko": [[[1, 4], [6]], [[7, 10], [13, 16, 17]], [[18, 19], [15]], [[21], [23]]]},

"schoenberg-database": {"non_row": "schoenberg-database",
 "kernel": "The Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts (SDBM) is an online, user-maintained, open-access database run by the Schoenberg Institute for Manuscript Studies at the University of Pennsylvania, aggregating historic observations of pre-modern manuscripts drawn from over 14,000 auction and sales catalogs, inventories, collection catalogs and other sources. Its unit is the observation: an entry in a source that documents a manuscript.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 3, 6],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It documents the sales and locations of manuscripts from around the world. At the core of its Institute is the Lawrence J. Schoenberg collection of manuscripts, donated to the Penn Libraries in 2011; the collection and the database are distinct.",
                  "It is built by its users: members are invited to contribute their \"own personal observations of a manuscript or group of manuscripts\" singly or by batch upload. It may be searched or browsed by author, title, seller, provenance and date, downloaded in .xlsx or .csv format, and queried through a public SPARQL endpoint."]),
  ("How it works", ["Each record is an observation of a manuscript at a point in time, in a sales or auction catalogue or a collection catalogue, and two or more observations of one manuscript are linked by a manuscript record. Its relations are sale, location and ownership.",
                    "Its carriers include a VIAF-based name authority, enhanced provenance data and a SPARQL endpoint; its provenance facet as read is headed by the Bibliothèque nationale de France (23,741), Sir Thomas Phillipps (21,582) and the British Library (7,822). The Institute is ineligible to participate in VIAF, and through Mapping Manuscript Migrations hundreds of names were corrected for authority control."]),
  ("Its counts", ["The counts are of sources and of records, each with its date: over 13,000 sources in 2015, over 14,000 in 2020 and on the home page as read, and more than 240,000 records of manuscript observations in May 2020. The home page's source-type facet gave 122,751 collection catalogues, 99,319 auction and dealer catalogues and 19,859 unpublished sources in 2020, and 129,525, 106,401 and 23,861 in 2026."]),
  ("What its selection leaves out", ["It tracks generic titles, and works in the specific sense FRBR employs are outside it; a manuscript's current location is not specifically present, though it can often be inferred from the most recent observation; and its entries for sales and gifts carried no place information. Six of the research questions Mapping Manuscript Migrations set could not be answered from it alone. Downloaded search results are offered under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 licence."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["It is one of the three source datasets of Mapping Manuscript Migrations, the 2017–2020 project that turned them into Linked Open Data without correcting them. At its address the Capture Registry judges Google's AI Overview \"The best-composed of the census rows,\" with host, unit, scale and structure at the database's own grain and only the licence, the condition of reuse, left out; the Institute and Penn Libraries are named, and no person or identifier, at a provenance erasure rate of 0.5."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 4, 5], [19, 20]], [[6, 8], [9, 10]], [[16, 17]], [[24, 21]], [[25, 27, 28]]]},

"vroniplag-wiki": {"non_row": "vroniplag-wiki",
 "kernel": "VroniPlag Wiki is a volunteer wiki, started on 28 March 2011 at Wikia (now Fandom), that examines and documents plagiarism in German doctoral theses and other academic writings. Its unit is a thesis, documented as fragments that set each passage beside the source it reproduces without attribution.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 3, 6],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It is named after the first thesis documented in it, that of Veronica Saß, daughter of the German politician Edmund Stoiber; \"Vroni\" is a German nickname for \"Veronica.\" It grew from the circle of the GuttenPlag Wiki, adopting that wiki's idea after the revocation of Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg's doctorate.",
                  "German Wikipedia's infobox names Martin Heidingsfelder as founder; by his own account he started it on 28 March 2011 under the pseudonym \"Goalgetter,\" and the project rejects the sole founding he claims for himself."]),
  ("How it works", ["Suspected plagiarism is first documented, anonymously if a user wishes; after verification the affected passages are divided into fragments that allow direct comparison with the originals, and each fragment \"is classified as to the kind of plagiarism and must be verified by a second person before it is acceptable.\" A published thesis has an overview with a bar code visualizing the amount of plagiarism, and the measure is the share of a thesis's pages found to contain plagiarism: Veronica Saß's stood at 53.98% on 25 May 2011, and her doctorate was rescinded at Konstanz."]),
  ("What it publishes and what followed", ["It publishes documentation openly, with the authors' names, only on theses found to contain serious plagiarism, and before publication the university's office of academic integrity is formally notified, since any revocation of title must be done by the university.",
                                           "By February 2016 it had examined 152 dissertations, 8 habilitations, a master's thesis and a book on scientific writing in detail; by 3 April 2023, 201 dissertations and 13 habilitation theses were named. As of 30 April 2024 at least 90 persons had lost the doctorate and two the habilitation after a documentation on the wiki. Some universities disagreed with its findings even where 40 to 70% of pages were affected."]),
  ("Criticism and reception", ["The criticism most often raised is its contributors' anonymity, which those involved justify by holding that in scholarship only what is said matters, and by their fear of professional disadvantage and intimidation. Critics also accused it of political motivation, which its contributors denied.",
                               "Visualization of the findings proved a key factor in its reception; it was the first crowdsourcing phenomenon of its kind in Germany to be referred to as an authoritative source in all major German media. Wikimedia Deutschland awarded it the Zedler Prize for Free Knowledge on 1 July 2012."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive places plagiarism detection beside its Provenance Erasure Rate as an adjacent integrity tool: it \"Detects copy; PER detects lineage erasure regardless of paraphrase.\" At the name, Google's AI Overview identified the wiki, its founding, its name, what it documents and its notice to the university, and left out its method of second-person verification, the revocations and the founder German Wikipedia names."])],
 "ko": [[[4, 5], [6]], [[9, 10, 11]], [[14], [16, 18, 19]], [[20, 21], [22, 23]], [[12, 25, 26]]]},

"getty-provenance-index": {"non_row": "getty-provenance-index",
 "kernel": "The Getty Provenance Index is the Getty Research Institute's database of art provenance: over 12 million digitized, open-access resources, drawn from archival records of the 16th to the 20th centuries, that document historic transfers of art ownership. Its unit is a resource drawn from a primary document, and the Index is moving to linked open data on the Arches platform.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 7, 18],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["One name holds two things: \"an active research initiative carried out by Getty staff that supports an evolving data resource with the same name.\" The records are used to trace \"the ownership, movement, and histories of artworks,\" with strengths in European and American art."]),
  ("What it holds", ["It draws on transcribed primary documents of three kinds. The dealer stock books are those of two firms: the 15 stock books of Goupil & Cie./Boussod, Valadon & Cie in Paris (1846–1919) and the 11 painting stock books of M. Knoedler & Co. in New York (1872–1970), each over 190,000 resources. The sales catalogs document works offered for sale in Europe from 1650 to 1945, over 12 million resources from more than 22,000 catalogs. The archival inventories, legal records of European private collections from 1520 to 1880, hold over 276,000 records from more than 13,000 documents.",
                     "Three further databases, the Collectors Files, Payments to Artists and Public Collections, complement the Index on its home page and form its foundation on its coverage page. They hold about 20,000 collectors' folders, about 1,000 payments recorded in Rome between 1576 and 1711, and over 95,000 public-collection records from 1500 to 1990."]),
  ("History", ["The Getty's own pages state the founding three ways: founded in 1974 by Burton Fredericksen in the Getty Museum's Paintings department; begun in the early 1980s as an initiative led by curator Burton B. Fredericksen; and \"1981 – present.\"",
               "First compiled digitally in 1984, published on CD-ROM in 1994 and launched online in 1998, it had become a dedicated department by 2004 and is headed now by Sandra van Ginhoven. The Remodeled Index on Arches holds the dealer stock books and sales catalogs; archival inventory data are to follow, and the original datasets remain in the Legacy Index."]),
  ("Access and limits", ["Machine access runs through API documentation with guidance on licences and attribution, and through CSV exports of the Legacy Index on GitHub. Its stated limit is coverage: \"The quantity and scope of research material available in the Getty Provenance Index® varies by region, period, and type of document.\""]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive counts art-historical provenance, the \"Documented chain of ownership and attribution for art objects,\" as an adjacent tradition to semantic provenance, with the \"Same conceptual structure; different object.\" At the name, Google's AI Overview composed the index with its scale and maintainer right and its origin left out: of the two founding statements on the Getty's pages it gives neither and names no person, and it joins the two firms' stock books under one count and one span, at a provenance erasure rate of 0.5."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3, 7]], [[9, 10, 11], [12, 13]], [[14], [16, 17, 18]], [[20, 21]], [[6, 25, 26]]]},

"olmotrace": {"non_row": "olmotrace",
 "kernel": "OLMoTrace is Ai2's tool that traces the outputs of language models back to their full, multi-trillion-token training data in real time, showing verbatim matches between spans of output and training documents. Introduced on 9 April 2025 as a feature of the Ai2 Playground, its unit is a maximal matching span of model output.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 3],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It highlights long and unique spans of a model's answer found at least once in the training data, verbatim matching being \"the most direct connection between LM outputs and the training data.\" Its purpose is to explore where models \"may have learned to generate certain word sequences,\" for researchers, developers and the general public.",
                  "Its 31 authors are at the Allen Institute for AI, the University of Washington, UC Berkeley and Stanford. It is publicly available and fully open-source, its core under the Apache 2.0 license, and it appeared in ACL 2025's System Demonstrations with the Best Demo award."]),
  ("How it works", ["It indexes the model's full training corpus in an extended infini-gram index: for OLMo-2-32B-Instruct, 3.2 billion documents and 4.6 trillion tokens across pre-, mid- and post-training. Up to 10 enclosing snippets are retrieved per kept span and ranked by BM25. Tracing takes 4.46 seconds per query on average, and the first document displayed averages 1.90 on a 0–3 relevance scale.",
                    "At launch it served OLMo 2 32B and 13B Instruct and OLMoE 1B 7B Instruct, and it can be applied to any model whose full training data the provider holds; it traces only models whose training data is open."]),
  ("What it does not claim", ["Its authors state the limit: \"The retrieved documents should not be interpreted as having a causal effect on the LM output, or as supporting evidence or citations for the LM output.\" Ai2 called the documents associations, and said its results were not \"indicators of authority.\"",
                              "Fact checking appears in the paper as a possibility, qualified by the warning that words mislead out of context and some web sources are unreliable."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The archive places it in the class of data attribution, which asks \"Which training examples shaped a specific output\" and \"Operates on training/output relation; PER operates on output/reader relation.\" Of that class it holds that partial tracing \"is possible at cost\" and does not recover authorship.",
                          "At its own name, Google's AI Overview held the tool, its maker, its scale and its verbatim matching, and gave as its first use fact-checking whether claims in a model's answer are backed by real training documents. The Capture Registry reads this as \"the conversion of a matching relation into a citation relation that the paper names and refuses.\""])],
 "ko": [[[3, 10], [23, 24, 25]], [[5, 6, 7], [8, 9]], [[17, 18], [15]], [[11, 12], [21, 22]]]},
}
